# {{WidgetName}}

`{{WidgetId}}` is the provider-neutral two-source WidgetRail embedded-media
starter. It keeps native controller controls outside one host-owned media plane,
uses the public compact pinned presentation, and has no provider, network,
credential, DOM, window, or browser authority.

## Build and verify

```powershell
dotnet build .\{{WidgetName}}.csproj --configuration Release
dotnet test --project .\tests\{{WidgetName}}.Tests.csproj `
  --configuration Release --no-ansi --progress off --output Detailed `
  --minimum-expected-tests 4
wrail preview . --scenario ready
wrail validate .
wrail pack . --configuration Release --output .\{{WidgetName}}-0.1.0.wrwidget
```

The generated tests use deterministic playback events: no WebView, provider,
account, network, or credential is involved. WidgetRail's own template gate also
runs the sealed adapter through the provider-neutral adapter-conformance harness.

## Runtime and resource staging are opt-in

`EmbeddedMediaAdapterRuntime.js` is the canonical SDK runtime. The host does not
inject it. This project explicitly takes it from the WidgetSdk NuGet
`contentFiles/any/any/WidgetRail` location and stages it as
`media/adapter-runtime.js`; `adapter.html` loads that exact relative file before
calling `WidgetRailEmbeddedMediaAdapter.create`. Keep the same staged path in
`EmbeddedMediaSession.Resources` with `application/javascript`.

The two MP4s are sealed, package-local example sources. Replace both the files
and their bounded key-to-path map together. Do not accept arbitrary paths or
URLs from actions or playback events.

## Command matrix

| Native control | Declared adapter message | Package command |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Play after user activation | `arm-activate` / `activate` | `Play` |
| Play or pause | `toggle` | `Play` or `Pause` |
| Timeline, LT, or RT seek | `seek`, `seek-back`, `seek-forward` | `Seek` |
| Volume slider | `volume` | `SetVolume` |
| Previous or next source | `previous`, `next` | `Load` |

`SeekStepSeconds` is package policy. Change it once; the normal buttons and the
host-owned compact pinned player use the same declared step. Add a command only
when the surface declaration, native action, adapter hook, and conformance case
all agree.

## Lifecycle cookbook

1. **First activation:** queue exactly one `Load` for the first bounded media
   key. The runtime waits for document initialization and owns command
   correlation; the widget does not poll or resend.
2. **One command in flight:** retain one `PendingCommand`. Every initiating
   control derives Busy from its exact kind. Ignore additional actions until the
   exact terminal event clears it; there is no backlog.
3. **Terminal admission:** accept a correlated terminal only when session ID,
   monotonic event sequence, command sequence, and bounded media key all match.
   Unsolicited observations use command sequence zero and may update current
   playback state, but never acknowledge a pending command.
4. **Route hide/show:** the library route publishes the same session declaration
   with no `MediaViewport`. An already-resident controller parks without teardown;
   a cold declaration remains dormant and creates no background controller.
   Returning publishes the exact viewport and loads only when no resident session
   exists.
5. **Provider error:** an exact error terminal clears its command and exposes a
   bounded error code. A prior resident session remains represented; an initial
   load failure does not invent residency or fake playback.
6. **Replacement:** changing the session identity, entry document, resource
   declaration, or controller generation retires the old document and all of
   its pending work. The shared runtime aborts driver hooks; package code must
   reject late events by current authority.
7. **Shutdown:** widget removal/restart, adapter failure, eviction, and host
   shutdown are terminal boundaries. Do not promise restoration unless the
   package deliberately persists provider-neutral durable state. Never persist
   a pending command or browser/controller identity.

Alternative presentations are declared explicitly through
`SupportedPresentations`. Remove `MediaPresentationKind.CompactPinned` to remove
only that host projection; the ordinary native full player and its command
authority remain.
The pinned surface owns X/LT/RT/B/View behavior through the declared generic
media contract—do not author a second pinned control tree.

